 {:seon.task/name "real-mb-reads-what-macos-charges"
  :seon.task/plan "Done (worktree session reuse, 2026-10-23). What existed: bin/check scale's root gauge, seon.lib/real-mb, read 398-404 MB for a root whose phys_footprint was 1,460 MB with the embedding model open, and 856 MB on Seon's own program without it (jolt/similar-finds-helpers-by-embedding). Cause, measured: real-mb summed the private and shared-now-private resident pages of the process's VM regions, and a fork turns the parent's pages copy-on-write, so they left the sum: a parent holding a 730 MB block read 76 MB after forking one child (its phys_footprint stayed 730 MB); root forks the fork server, so it read about a quarter of what it holds. The note that macOS's footprint overstates a forked child does not hold for the task's own ledger: a child read 3 MB at the fork and 43 MB after writing 30 MB of the shared block. Fixed: real-mb is the task's phys_footprint (task_info TASK_VM_INFO: private dirty pages, compressed ones included, what Activity Monitor and jetsam charge), for root, the fork server and each agent; native/probe.c answers the ledger only, and its region walk (~1.5 ms a read) is deleted. bin/check scale's need reads it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.lib-test/the-memory-gauge-reads-what-macos-charges-across-a-fork}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-fork-from-a-threaded-process-never-hangs"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-25 by root's bin/test --all on a clean main (888a728e2): shard 3 hung 61 minutes. Its runner runs tests concurrently, on threads, and the last started were seon.lib-test/a-fork-beside-a-running-thread-is-refused-and-says-how-many-run and seon.lib-test/a-forked-child-runs-go-blocks-and-makes-a-store. A forked child of the runner (pid 4782) sat at 0% CPU, its main thread in S_condition_wait -> _pthread_mutex_firstfit_lock_wait -> __psynch_mutexwait: it inherited a Chez mutex locked by a thread that does not exist in the child. The cause is measured (design agent-loop section 8, docs/failure-points.md#physics-who-may-run-threads): ```seon.lib/fork!``` refuses only when another Chez thread is ACTIVE, but a thread is inactive while it still holds a channel's mutex on its way into and out of S_condition_wait (reference-code/chez/c/thread.c:709-720). Measured in an agent with 8 go blocks busy: 199 of 200 forks refused and 50 of 50 forced through deadlocked. So the refusal misses exactly the threads a concurrent runner starts; the static fork server (part 1 of the agent loop) is about memory and does not touch this class. What removes it: a process that forks is one OS thread when it forks. Agents now are (Jolt's main carrier, jolt.host/main-carrier-run!: 50 of 50 forks mid-traffic exit 0). The test runner is not: a test that forks runs, in a runner with other threads, beside them. Plan: (1) ```seon.lib/fork!``` refuses when the process has any other OS thread (```seon.lib/nthreads```), with the count and that reason, except the one call that may (root forking the fork server at boot, its threads parked and quiet: say so where it is called); (2) the runner runs a test that forks in a process of its own that is one thread (a fork-server-style child of the shard, or the shard under jolt.host/main-carrier-run!), never beside concurrent tests; (3) the two lib-test tests above run there. Deletes nothing; replaces the active-thread count as the fork's guard."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.lib-test/a-fork-beside-a-running-thread-is-refused-and-says-how-many-run seon.lib-test/a-forked-child-runs-go-blocks-and-makes-a-store}}]
